--TEST--
`Core\Sse` refuses the four events and heads a client could not act on, each named in its own message rather than framed and sent anyway — `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate` and `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The refusals belong to the framing and not to the member, because each of
// them is a way the framing could silently stop being true. They are reported
// here, where the program that wrote the event is.

// A client dispatches nothing for an empty data buffer, so an empty payload is
// an event that provably could not arrive.
var $empty = "";
try {
    Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
        var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
        $sse->send("");
    });
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    $empty = $refused->message;
}
echo $empty, "\n";

// Stripping the line break would change the name an application dispatches on,
// which is the silent wrong answer a refusal exists instead of.
var $named = "";
try {
    Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
        var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
        $sse->send("sold", event: "order\ndata: forged");
    });
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    $named = $refused->message;
}
echo $named, "\n";

// A NUL is refused with the two line breaks because its effect is the same: a
// client discards an id containing one outright, so what the application wrote
// is not what the peer acts on.
var $identified = "";
try {
    Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
        var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
        $sse->send("sold", id: "42\u{0}9");
    });
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    $identified = $refused->message;
}
echo $identified, "\n";

// `Core\Response::stream` takes any status; this refusal is the event stream's
// alone. An event stream is `200` by protocol, so a program that set a status
// and then opened one said two things about one response.
var $contradicted = "";
try {
    Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
        Core\Response::setStatus(404);
        Core\Sse::stream();
    });
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    $contradicted = $refused->message;
}
echo $contradicted, "\n";

// A payload that is not a `string` goes through the encoder `Core\Json::encode`
// is, so a value that encoder has no document for is refused here in the same
// sentence it would be refused there.
var $unencodable = "";
try {
    Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
        var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
        $sse->send(new Ticket());
    });
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    $unencodable = $refused->message;
}
echo $unencodable, "\n";

/// A class that does not participate in JSON, which is the default: nothing
/// crosses the encoder without `#[Json\Derive]` saying which fields may.
class Ticket
{
    public string $id = "t-1";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Sse::send(): an event needs a payload: a client dispatches nothing for an empty data buffer, so this event could not arrive
Core\Sse::send(): an event name cannot carry a line break or a NUL: a client dispatches on this name, and either byte would end the event early
Core\Sse::send(): an event id cannot carry a line break or a NUL: either byte would end the event early, and a client discards an id containing a NUL outright
Core\Sse::stream(): this request set the status `404` and an event stream is `200` by protocol, so the two cannot both be answered
Core\Sse::send(): the payload cannot be encoded: an instance of `Ticket` has no JSON encoding — a class participates by carrying `#[Json\Derive]` or by writing `toJson` itself
